Subject: [Vita] Make localtime_r usable, and never fstring an unassigned zone

MRI's LOCALTIME_OVERFLOW_PROBLEM check runs mktime over what localtime_r
returned and rejects the result when the two disagree. On this device they
always disagree: VitaSDK's localtime_r converts through
sceRtcConvertUtcToLocalTime (the console's own time zone) while newlib's
mktime reads only TZ, which nothing here sets, so mktime answers as if the
fields were UTC. rb_localtime_r therefore returned NULL for every timestamp,
localtimew fell through to guess_local_offset, and its "last resort" block ran
with `VALUE zone;` never assigned: rb_fstring dereferenced that garbage and
stopped the rgss thread with a data abort (stop 0x30004) at rb_fstring+0x18,
string.c:409, called from time.c:1485. That crash was the first thing a file-I/O test run hit when it rendered a
Time, and any game interpolating one would have hit it.

Drop the round trip on Vita, where localtime_r already reports an unusable t
as NULL, and give the last-resort block its UTC answer so a localtime_r
failure can no longer produce a wild VALUE or read an unset struct tm.
Evidence: a hardware crash dump.

--- a/time.c
+++ b/time.c
@@ -706,7 +706,13 @@
 	if (tmp) *result = *tmp;
     }
 #endif
-#if defined(HAVE_MKTIME) && defined(LOCALTIME_OVERFLOW_PROBLEM)
+/* Vita: localtime_r converts through sceRtcConvertUtcToLocalTime, i.e. the
+ * console's own time zone, while newlib's mktime reads only the TZ variable,
+ * which nothing on this device sets (so: UTC). The round trip below therefore
+ * disagrees by the console offset for every t and rejected every local time.
+ * VitaSDK's localtime_r already returns NULL when sceRtc refuses t, which is
+ * the overflow this check exists to catch. */
+#if defined(HAVE_MKTIME) && defined(LOCALTIME_OVERFLOW_PROBLEM) && !defined(__vita__)
     if (result) {
         long gmtoff1 = 0;
         long gmtoff2 = 0;
@@ -1478,10 +1484,14 @@
         static int now_isdst = 0;
         static VALUE now_zone;
         if (now == 0) {
-            VALUE zone;
+            /* On a platform where localtime_r fails for every t this is
+             * reached with nothing assigned: zone stayed uninitialised and
+             * rb_fstring dereferenced a garbage VALUE, and tm was read
+             * uninitialised. The statics already hold the UTC answer. */
+            VALUE zone = str_utc;
             now = time(NULL);
-            localtime_with_gmtoff_zone(&now, &tm, &now_gmtoff, &zone);
-            now_isdst = tm.tm_isdst;
+            if (localtime_with_gmtoff_zone(&now, &tm, &now_gmtoff, &zone))
+                now_isdst = tm.tm_isdst;
             zone = rb_fstring(zone);
             rb_gc_register_mark_object(zone);
             now_zone = zone;
